= Texas-Style Smoked Beef Brisket
:author: Pitmaster Gemini
:toc: left
:icons: font
:custom-style: barbecue

The holy grail of backyard barbecue. This recipe focuses on the traditional Central Texas method: low and slow, heavy on the black pepper, and smoked over oak.

image::brisket.jpg[Perfect Smoked Brisket, 400, 300]

Prep Time & Yield
[cols="1,1", options="header"]
|===
| Metric | Value
| Prep Time | 45 minutes (plus overnight dry brine)
| Cook Time | 10 to 14 hours (depending on size)
| Rest Time | 2 to 4 hours
| Yield | 10 - 12 Servings
|===

Ingredients
1 Whole Packer Beef Brisket (12-14 lbs, Choice or Prime grade)

1/4 cup Coarse Kosher Salt

1/4 cup 16-Mesh Coarse Black Pepper

1 tbsp Granulated Garlic (optional)

A few splashes Worcestershire sauce or yellow mustard (as a binder)

Equipment Needed
Smoker (Offset, pellet, or Weber Smokey Mountain)

Oak wood chunks or logs

Sharp trimming knife (boning knife preferred)

Pink butcher paper (or heavy-duty aluminum foil)

Digital meat thermometer

Step-by-Step Instructions
1. The Trim
Trimming ensures even cooking and prevents burnt edges.

Place the brisket on a large cutting board, fat cap side up.

Trim the fat cap down to a uniform 1/4-inch thickness.

Flip and remove the hard, dense chunk of fat (the kernel) located between the point and the flat.

Square up the edges of the flat to create a aerodynamic shape for airflow.

2. The Rub
The classic Texas "Dalmatian" rub.

[IMPORTANT]
Size matters for the pepper! Standard table-ground black pepper is too fine and will make the bark bitter. Use 16-mesh coarse pepper for that authentic texture.
Lightly coat the entire brisket with your binder (mustard or Worcestershire).

Mix the salt, pepper, and garlic in a shaker.

Apply the rub generously from about 12 inches above the meat to ensure even coverage.

Optional: Let it sit uncovered in the fridge overnight to dry brine.

3. The Smoke
  [Smoker] -------------------> 225°F - 250°F (107°C - 121°C)
     |
     +--> Target Internal Temp: ~203°F (95°C)
Preheat your smoker to 225°F - 250°F and add your oak wood.

Place the brisket in the smoker, fat cap facing your heat source (usually fat-side up on offsets, fat-side down on pellet grills).

Smoke undisturbed for the first 4 to 5 hours until a dark, mahogany "bark" forms.

4. The Wrap (The "Texas Crutch")
Around an internal temperature of 165°F, the meat will hit a "stall" where evaporation cools the meat and stops temperature rise.

Wrap the brisket tightly in pink butcher paper.

Return it to the smoker, seam-side down.

Why butcher paper? It breathes better than foil, keeping your hard-earned bark crunchy instead of soggy.

5. The Finish and Rest
"It's done when it's done, not when the clock says so."

Cook until the internal temperature reaches roughly 203°F, but more importantly, until your thermometer probe slides into the thickest part of the flat like warm butter.

Remove from the smoker, leave it wrapped, and place it in an empty cooler lined with towels.

Rest for at least 2 hours. This allows the juices to redistribute throughout the meat.

Slicing & Serving
Always slice against the grain.

Remember that the grain of the flat runs in a different direction than the point. Separate them or adjust your slicing angle halfway through.

Slices should be about the thickness of a pencil.
